### What is the ASCII case conversion trick?

In Java, a `char` is a number representing its Unicode code point. For the basic Latin letters, the code points match the ASCII table. Uppercase A is 65, lowercase a is 97, and the difference is exactly 32. This holds for every letter: B is 66 and b is 98, Z is 90 and z is 122. That constant gap is what makes manual case conversion possible with simple arithmetic.

```java
package com.bank.util;

public class AsciiGapDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("A = " + (int) 'A');  // 65
        System.out.println("a = " + (int) 'a');  // 97
        System.out.println("Gap = " + ('a' - 'A')); // 32
        System.out.println("Z = " + (int) 'Z');  // 90
        System.out.println("z = " + (int) 'z');  // 122
    }
}
```

```text
A = 65
a = 97
Gap = 32
Z = 90
z = 122
```

### Why does this matter in banking?

The National Bank of India stores internal account codes in a single canonical case so that lookups match regardless of how a clerk typed them. When Rajesh enters code "hdfc2001" and Priya enters "HDFC2001", both must resolve to the same record. The system normalizes to uppercase, and the digits in the code are left untouched by the range check.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.util.ManualCaseConverter;

public class AccountCodeNormalizer {
    public static String normalize(String code) {
        return ManualCaseConverter.toUpperCase(code);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(normalize("hdfc2001")); // HDFC2001
        System.out.println(normalize("HDFC2001")); // HDFC2001
        System.out.println(normalize("Hdfc2001")); // HDFC2001
    }
}
```

```text
HDFC2001
HDFC2001
HDFC2001
```

### How do you implement toUpperCase manually?

Loop over each character. If the character is in the lowercase range from 'a' to 'z', subtract 32 to get the uppercase letter. Otherwise, append the character unchanged. Build the result in a StringBuilder.

```java
package com.bank.util;

public class ManualCaseConverter {
    public static String toUpperCase(String input) {
        if (input == null) return null;
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(input.length());
        for (int i = 0; i < input.length(); i++) {
            char c = input.charAt(i);
            if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') {
                sb.append((char) (c - 32));
            } else {
                sb.append(c);
            }
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(toUpperCase("savings account 2001"));
    }
}
```

```text
SAVINGS ACCOUNT 2001
```

### How do you implement toLowerCase manually?

The mirror operation: if the character is in the uppercase range from 'A' to 'Z', add 32 to get the lowercase letter. Everything else stays unchanged.

```java
package com.bank.util;

public class ManualToLower {
    public static String toLowerCase(String input) {
        if (input == null) return null;
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(input.length());
        for (int i = 0; i < input.length(); i++) {
            char c = input.charAt(i);
            if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z') {
                sb.append((char) (c + 32));
            } else {
                sb.append(c);
            }
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(toLowerCase("SAVINGS ACCOUNT 2001"));
    }
}
```

```text
savings account 2001
```

### Why is the range check critical?

Without the range check, subtracting 32 from a digit or a space corrupts the character. The space character is 32, so subtracting 32 would turn it into the null character (0). The digit '5' is 53, and subtracting 32 produces '!' (33). The range check ensures only actual letters are transformed.

```java
package com.bank.util;

public class RangeCheckDemo {
    // WRONG: no range check
    public static String wrongUpper(String s) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(s.length());
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            sb.append((char) (s.charAt(i) - 32));
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String input = "hdfc 2001";
        System.out.println("Wrong:   '" + wrongUpper(input) + "'");
        System.out.println("Correct: '" + ManualCaseConverter.toUpperCase(input) + "'");
    }
}
```

```text
Wrong:   'HDFC\u00002001' (corrupted space and digits)
Correct: 'HDFC 2001'
```

### What are the limitations of this approach?

This trick only works for the 26 ASCII Latin letters A to Z and a to z. It fails on accented characters like e with acute (code 233), other alphabets like Devanagari, and locale-specific rules. The most famous example is the Turkish dotless i: in Turkish, uppercase of 'i' is not 'I' but a dotted capital I, and the built-in `toUpperCase(Locale)` handles this correctly while the manual trick does not.

```java
package com.bank.util;

import java.util.Locale;

public class LocaleLimitDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String name = "istanbul";

        // Manual trick: always produces 'I'
        System.out.println("Manual: " + ManualCaseConverter.toUpperCase(name));

        // Built-in with Turkish locale: produces dotted I
        System.out.println("Turkish: " + name.toUpperCase(new Locale("tr")));
        System.out.println("English: " + name.toUpperCase(Locale.ENGLISH));
    }
}
```

```text
Manual: ISTANBUL
Turkish: ISTANBUL
English: ISTANBUL
```

### How does this compare to the built-in methods?

| Aspect | Manual (subtract/add 32) | String.toUpperCase(Locale) |
|---|---|---|
| Works for ASCII A-Z | Yes | Yes |
| Works for accented chars | No | Yes |
| Locale-aware | No | Yes |
| Performance | Fastest | Slightly slower |
| Use case | Internal ASCII codes | User-facing text |

```java
package com.bank.util;

import java.util.Locale;

public class ComparisonDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String code = "hdfc2001";
        System.out.println("Manual:   " + ManualCaseConverter.toUpperCase(code));
        System.out.println("Built-in: " + code.toUpperCase(Locale.ENGLISH));
    }
}
```

```text
Manual:   HDFC2001
Built-in: HDFC2001
```

### What are common mistakes?

The first mistake is forgetting the range check, which corrupts non-letter characters. The second mistake is using this trick on user-facing names with accented characters. The third mistake is not building with StringBuilder and instead concatenating strings in a loop, which creates a new String object on every iteration.

```java
package com.bank.util;

public class CommonMistakesDemo {
    // WRONG: string concatenation in loop creates many objects
    public static String slowUpper(String s) {
        String result = "";
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            char c = s.charAt(i);
            if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') {
                result += (char) (c - 32);
            } else {
                result += c;
            }
        }
        return result; // O(n^2) due to string concatenation
    }

    // CORRECT: StringBuilder
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Slow: " + slowUpper("hdfc2001"));
        System.out.println("Fast: " + ManualCaseConverter.toUpperCase("hdfc2001"));
    }
}
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

The loop runs in O(n) time and uses O(n) space for the StringBuilder. Each character is visited exactly once and the arithmetic operation is constant time. String concatenation in a loop would degrade to O(n squared) because each concatenation creates a new String and copies all previous characters.

